> today, but at least part of the randomness is in the monster movement.

> With the current version, you can still get unlucky a few times in a
> row - e.g. when Fast:
>     if (rn2(3) != 0) moveamt += NORMAL_SPEED / 2;
> can fail you three times in a row with probability 1/27, and then
> *all* nearby monsters can take advantage of it simultaneously.

Now that's an interesting data point. So it does sound as Pat's proposal
(or something like it) would have less of an effect on the outcome of a
short battle than the previous change (to hero movement). That's very
encouraging.

> There are other interesting effects as well - consider for example
> ##qqqq#####+.......@...|; if all the rothes move synchronously, they
> get to the hero as one pack - randomization could make them separate.

Yes. Clearly the first rothe will move at "normal" speed. Just as clearly
the second rothe will move at something less than "normal" speed because
there's a chance at each move that it will be blocked by the first rothe.
I suspect that with an infinitely long corridor the rothes would end up
seperated by a fraction of a square on average, with that fraction being
determined by the random element. My gut feeling would be that this would
not be a problem if the fraction was low enough. I base that on the premis
that if the hero sees a queue of monsters coming to attack him with the
odd space in between then that simply allows him the possibility of a
strategic withdrawal, which can only improve the game. If it got to the
point where the hero was seeing monsters approaching in alternate squares
it would start to look very artificial.

